16:25:04 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: The Federal Insurance Contributions Act (FICA) tax is an equitable method of funding Social Security and Medicare because it operates as a social insurance system that ties contributions directly to benefits, distributes the tax burden broadly, and incorporates progressive elements in its benefit calculations. First, FICA is a proportional tax up to the annual wage cap (currently $168,600 for Social Security), meaning all workers pay the same percentage—6.2% for Social Security and 1.45% for Medicare (with an additional 0.9% for high earners). This uniformity ensures that everyone who earns wages contributes, creating a broad and stable funding base. Unlike general income taxes, which can be avoided through deductions or tax shelters, FICA is withheld automatically from wages, making it difficult to evade. This inclusivity is fair because every worker benefits from Social Security and Medicare in retirement or upon disability. Second, the system is designed as insurance, not redistribution. Benefits are calculated based on lifetime earnings, so those who contribute more receive higher benefits—though the formula is mildly progressive, replacing a higher percentage of pre-retirement income for lower earners. This balances individual equity with social adequacy. The wage cap also limits the maximum benefit, ensuring that high earners don’t receive disproportionate returns. Meanwhile, Medicare’s 2.9% tax (combined employer-employee) has no cap, and high earners pay an extra 0.9%, adding progressivity. Critics often argue that FICA is regressive because the wage cap means high earners pay a lower percentage of total income. However, this overlooks that benefits are also capped—high earners receive a smaller replacement rate. Moreover, the employer share (half of the tax) is economically borne partly by workers through reduced wages, but this is a feature of any payroll tax. The alternative—funding these programs through general